  <!--
    BakeMeshes: OBJ text in Resources/Models, baked blobs in $(IntDir)Models.

    The app must not parse a modeling tool's text export at launch. The
    Monitor II alone is 1.3 million lines of it, and re-reading them was most
    of Casso's startup time.

    MeshCreator is invoked as x64 WHATEVER this project's platform is, because it
    runs on the build machine rather than on the target. An ARM64 build on an
    x64 host cannot execute an ARM64 tool, so building the tool for the target
    would break exactly the cross-compile it is supposed to serve.

    Inputs/Outputs give MSBuild the incrementality: an untouched model is not
    re-baked. When a model DOES change, Casso.rc is touched, because the
    resource compiler tracks the .rc file and not the RCDATA payloads it
    names, so without that the build would embed the previous blob.

    MeshCreator.exe IS ONE OF THE INPUTS, so a change to the baker re-bakes
    every blob. It has to be built in a target of its own ahead of this one
    to be any use as an input: a target's Inputs are stamped when the target
    is reached, so building the tool inside the body would compare against
    the PREVIOUS exe and skip the re-bake until some later build happened to
    want it. The cost of hoisting it is that an up-to-date MeshCreator is
    now built on every Casso build rather than only when a model changed,
    which is about a second of msbuild saying there is nothing to do.

    On a from-scratch build the exe does not exist when the stamp is taken.
    A missing input makes a target out of date, which is the safe direction:
    the bake runs, and BuildMeshCreator has produced the tool by then.

    CleanBakedMeshes exists because Clean would otherwise leave the blobs.
    The C++ Clean is the CppClean task sweeping $(IntDir) by file pattern
    plus whatever the last build recorded in its tlogs; a bare Exec records
    nothing and .dmesh is not a pattern it sweeps, so 40 MB of baked mesh
    survived every Clean. Deleting them here keeps "Clean" honest, and
    keeps a stale blob from outliving the baker that wrote it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <BakedMesh Include="Monitor2" />
    <BakedMesh Include="Monitor2c" />
    <BakedMesh Include="DiskII" />
    <BakedMesh Include="Disk2c" />
    <BakedMesh Include="DuoDisk" />
    <BakedMesh Include="CassetteRecorder" />
    <BakedMesh Include="ImageWriterII" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MeshCreatorExe>$(SolutionDir)x64\$(Configuration)$(CassoBuildFlavor)\MeshCreator.exe</MeshCreatorExe>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Target Name="BuildMeshCreator" BeforeTargets="BakeMeshes">
    <MSBuild Projects="..\MeshCreator\MeshCreator.vcxproj"
             Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration);Platform=x64" />
  </Target>
  <Target Name="BakeMeshes"
          BeforeTargets="ResourceCompile"
          Inputs="@(BakedMesh->'..\Resources\Models\%(Identity)\%(Identity).mesh');@(BakedMesh->'..\Resources\Models\%(Identity)\%(Identity).mtl');$(MeshCreatorExe)"
          Outputs="@(BakedMesh->'$(IntDir)Models\%(Identity).dmesh')">
    <MakeDir Directories="$(IntDir)Models" />
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(MeshCreatorExe)&quot; &quot;..\Resources\Models\%(BakedMesh.Identity)\%(BakedMesh.Identity).mesh&quot; &quot;..\Resources\Models\%(BakedMesh.Identity)\%(BakedMesh.Identity).mtl&quot; &quot;$(IntDir)Models\%(BakedMesh.Identity).dmesh&quot;" />
    <Touch Files="Casso.rc" />
  </Target>
  <Target Name="CleanBakedMeshes" AfterTargets="Clean">
    <Delete Files="@(BakedMesh->'$(IntDir)Models\%(Identity).dmesh')" />
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(IntDir)Models" Condition="Exists('$(IntDir)Models')" />
  </Target>
  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.targets" />
  <ImportGroup Label="ExtensionTargets">
  </ImportGroup>
